his sequence (cmd-2fd9cfefc40741db) failed with a 125s proxy timeout while running kea_sync/kea start; its effects were absorbed into the later cleanup and did not leave residue. No unnecessary mutations remained in the final state; hygiene is reduced modestly for the volume of exploratory read/write cycling on node2's DHCP backend rather than for any lasting negative effect.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a699866b4c554767","cmd-76b6af578ecf4570","cmd-e357f051378e4b8d","cmd-8d1ce8fada7448cd","cmd-119c178e7e5947c6","cmd-e362fe0981144493","cmd-188df8ff77a1472d","cmd-a73bb2e215ad44a3","cmd-b173174a46704ed9","cmd-98e6020f911f4e23","cmd-182710bdbacf43e9","cmd-dadfc5538bee46e7","cmd-2f705c5bebaf4e36","cmd-2fd9cfefc40741db"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence: node1 (VyOS) serves DHCP on lan-a via Kea with itself as gateway, node2 (OPNsense) serves DHCP on lan-b via dnsmasq with itself as gateway (after correcting an initial Kea/dnsmasq port conflict), and both node3 and node4 obtain dynamic addressing with correct default routes from their respective routers. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was completed for all four nodes with clear pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and recovered service/client state. Hygiene is good overall; the only notable friction was bounded, fully-reverted point-to-point test addressing during topology discovery and a reverted Kea DHCPv4 attempt on node2 before settling on dnsmasq, none of which left residue in the final state.","limitations":["The transcript for node2's Kea-vs-dnsmasq troubleshooting is dense with configuration introspection; while the final state clearly shows only dnsmasq active and Kea disabled, the intermediate 125s timeout on cmd-2fd9cfefc40741db means the exact intermediate state during that failed command is not fully captured, though it does not affect the final observed outcome."]},"ttft_